.TH REFFECTOR 1 "2026-08-14" "reffector" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
reffector \- report statically reachable side effects of Go functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B reffector
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ packages ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reffector
analyzes Go functions and methods and prints their possible externally
observable interactions, including effects inherited through function calls.
It does not modify the analyzed source or execute application functions,
initializers, or tests.
It performs analysis only, not automated refactoring.
.PP
Package arguments use Go package-pattern syntax, resolved by
.BR golang.org/x/tools/go/packages .
Examples include
.BR . ,
.BR ./billing ,
.BR ./... ,
.BR ./internal/... ,
and full import paths.
With no arguments, the current package is selected.
Dependencies are inspected as needed, but functions displayed belong to
selected packages, not ordinarily their dependencies.
The current GOOS, GOARCH, Go toolchain, and build constraints determine which
files are loaded.
.PP
Module loading uses
.BR -mod=readonly ,
even if GOFLAGS requests a different module mode.
Vendor mode is not used.
Missing module metadata produces an error instead of repairs to go.mod or
go.sum.
Workspace metadata is copied to temporary files, with paths rebased, so Go
checksum updates do not alter the original go.work.sum.
The trusted Go toolchain may download dependencies or toolchains and write
its caches outside the analyzed source tree.
.SH OPTIONS
Flags precede package arguments.
.TP
.B -direct
Report only effects attributed directly to each function, rather than all
transitively reachable effects.
.TP
.B -tests
Include functions defined in internal and external test files in selected
packages.
Ordinary and test variants are merged by source function identity.
Generated test executables are not displayed.
Tests and test initializers are not run.
Without this option, test files are excluded.
.TP
.B -json
Write a JSON array instead of text pairs.
.TP
.BR -h ", " -help
Print usage to standard error and exit successfully.
.SH OUTPUT
Text output contains one unique function/effect pair per line:
.PP
.nf
example.com/app.Load fs.read
example.com/app.Process fs.read
example.com/app.(*Store).Save db.exec
.fi
.PP
Functions are qualified with full package import paths.
Methods include their receiver type, including pointer receivers.
Anonymous functions append lexical suffixes such as
.BR $1 .
Explicit initialization functions have SSA-numbered names, such as
.BR init#1 .
Synthetic
.B init
represents package variable initialization and effects inherited from imported
package initialization.
Functions without identified effects are omitted.
.PP
Records are sorted by package import path, function identity, and effect.
Identical pairs are deduplicated.
No explanations or scores are written in normal output.
Diagnostics go to standard error.
.PP
JSON output is an array of objects with these fields:
.TP
.B package
Full package import path.
.TP
.B function
Package-local function identity, including the receiver for methods.
.TP
.B effect
Effect identifier from the vocabulary below.
.TP
.B direct
Boolean: true for an effect attributed directly to this function; false for
an inherited effect.
If the same effect is both direct and inherited, only one record is emitted,
with this field true.
.PP
No results produce empty text output or the JSON array
.BR [] .
An absent record is not proof that a function is pure.
.SH SIDE EFFECTS
Assignments to local variables are not effects.
The version-one vocabulary concentrates on externally observable operations:
.TP
.B fs.read
Read filesystem contents or metadata.
.TP
.B fs.write
Modify filesystem contents, metadata, or state, including writes through
an os.File handle.
The actual handle may refer to a device, pipe, or standard output.
.TP
.B fs.create
Create filesystem entries.
.TP
.B fs.remove
Remove filesystem entries.
.TP
.B net.connect
Initiate a network connection.
.TP
.B net.listen
Listen for or accept network connections.
.TP
.B process.exec
Start an external process.
.TP
.B env.read
Read process environment values.
.TP
.B env.write
Modify process environment values.
.TP
.B db.query
Execute a database query operation.
.TP
.B db.exec
Execute a database command operation, including summarized transaction and
prepare operations.
.TP
.B unknown.call
Reach an unresolved or opaque call whose effects cannot be reliably resolved,
or an interaction outside the supported vocabulary.
Examples include reflection invocation, assembly, cgo, unresolved function
parameters, and built-in print operations.
.PP
Known APIs are recognized by resolved package and receiver identities, not
names such as Read or Query alone.
The centralized table in internal/effects/effects.go is the authoritative list.
It includes common os filesystem and environment operations, os.File and
os.Root methods, selected path/filepath and io/ioutil operations,
net and crypto/tls connection/listener operations, net/http client/server
operations, os/exec process operations, and database/sql queries and commands.
.PP
An operation may have several classifications.
For example, os.WriteFile can create and write a file.
The flags passed to os.OpenFile are not evaluated: reading, writing, and
creation are all reported.
The operation exec.Command can inspect PATH and filesystem metadata;
Cmd.Run and Cmd.Start add process.exec.
Database queries are summarized as db.query without inferring a network
connection, because drivers need not use a network.
.SH DIRECT AND TRANSITIVE EFFECTS
A call to a recognized API summary is an effect directly attributed to the
calling function.
An unresolved call is also directly attributed at its call site.
A call to an ordinary helper inherits that helper's reachable effects.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.nf
func Load() {
    _, _ = os.ReadFile("config.json")
}
func Process() {
    Load()
}
.fi
.PP
Default results include both functions:
.PP
.nf
example.com/app.Load fs.read
example.com/app.Process fs.read
.fi
.PP
With
.BR -direct ,
only Load is reported.
More generally, reachable effects are a function's direct effects union the
reachable effects of its callees.
A monotone worklist reaches a stable result even for recursive and mutually
recursive functions.
.SH EXAMPLES
Analyze the current package, including inherited effects:
.PP
.nf
reffector .
.fi
.PP
Analyze all packages under the current directory:
.PP
.nf
reffector ./...
.fi
.PP
Report only direct effects in internal packages:
.PP
.nf
reffector -direct ./internal/...
.fi
.PP
Include tests and write machine-readable output:
.PP
.nf
reffector -tests -json ./billing
.fi
.PP
Analyze an import path available in the current Go environment:
.PP
.nf
reffector example.com/project/billing
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
A statically reachable effect means the analysis found a possible path to an
operation, not that it will execute.
This is not a purity proof, a security score, or a complete effect system.
No paths, URLs, destinations, permissions, or resource boundaries are inferred.
Shared-memory mutation is outside the vocabulary.
.PP
Typed SSA provides static calls, methods, closures, local function values,
phi merges, instantiated generics, and calls across packages.
Both deferred and goroutine calls are followed.
The graph is demand-driven from selected functions, including unused ones;
it does not require a main function.
.PP
Known concrete interface values are resolved to their methods.
Open interface calls use class-hierarchy analysis: all compatible concrete
types found in loaded source and SSA runtime types are possible receivers.
These calls also receive unknown.call because implementations outside the
loaded set may exist.
This works on libraries, but may produce false positives from types never
actually used at a call site.
It is not a context-sensitive points-to analysis.
.PP
Function values flowing through memory, parameters, or returned values are
reported as unknown rather than resolved interprocedurally.
Known local values and closures can be resolved.
Reflection invocation is opaque.
Assembly, cgo, and functions without Go bodies are opaque.
Code outside the loaded Go program is not inspected.
.PP
API summaries stop traversal into the summarized implementation.
They describe primary operation kinds, not every possible secondary effect
of custom drivers, hooks, or implementations.
HTTP summaries additionally include unknown.call for hidden hooks or handlers.
Specific effects of callbacks hidden behind summary boundaries or unresolved
value flows may therefore be missed.
unknown.call does not enumerate or bound those missing effects.
.PP
The analysis does not evaluate branch feasibility or argument values and may
report effects on paths impossible at runtime.
Its API table is intentionally incomplete.
Build-selected files and dependencies are analyzed for the current environment,
not every platform, optional plugin, or build tag.
Failure to identify an effect must never be interpreted as proof of purity.
.PP
Loading invokes trusted Go tools; this command is not a sandbox for malicious
toolchains, package drivers, or build environments.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Analysis and output succeeded, including when effects are found or no records
are produced; also successful help requests.
.TP
.B 1
Package loading, analysis, or output failed.
Loading errors prevent partial normal output.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line flags.
